theory.intro:
A moving particle's position vector $\underset{\sim}{r}(t)=x(t)\underset{\sim}{i}+y(t)\underset{\sim}{j}$ has an $x$ part and a $y$ part. Differentiate each part separately with respect to $t$ to get **velocity** $\underset{\sim}{v}(t)$, then again to get **acceleration** $\underset{\sim}{a}(t)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-3.4,0) -- (3.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-0.6) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-3:3, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.25*\x*\x});
\node[right] at (-3.3,3.0) {path of the particle};
\coordinate (P) at (2,1);
\fill (P) circle (2pt);
\node[below right] at (P) {$P$};
\draw[-{Stealth}, thick] (P) -- (3.2,2.2) node[right] {$\underset{\sim}{v}$};
\draw[-{Stealth}, thick] (P) -- (2,2.3) node[above] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
$\begin{aligned}\underset{\sim}{v}(t)&=-12\sin(4t)\underset{\sim}{i}+12\cos(4t)\underset{\sim}{j}\\|\underset{\sim}{v}(t)|&=\sqrt{144\sin^2(4t)+144\cos^2(4t)}\\&=\sqrt{144}\\&=12\ \text{m s}^{-1}\end{aligned}$
The speed is independent of $t$, so it is constant.
$\begin{aligned}\underset{\sim}{a}(t)&=-48\cos(4t)\underset{\sim}{i}-48\sin(4t)\underset{\sim}{j}\\&=-16\underset{\sim}{r}(t)\\\underset{\sim}{v}(t)\cdot\underset{\sim}{a}(t)&=576\sin(4t)\cos(4t)-576\cos(4t)\sin(4t)\\&=0\end{aligned}$
Both vectors are non-zero, so the zero dot product establishes perpendicularity. Acceleration points towards the centre; velocity is tangent to the circle.
At $t=0$, the diagram shows the given position and the derived directions. The arrows show directions, not vector magnitudes.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-3.7,0)--(4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3.7)--(0,4.7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black] (0,0) circle (3);
\fill[black] (3,0) circle (1.5pt);
\path (3,0) node[below right] {$P(3,0)$};
\path (0,0) node[below left] {$O$};
\draw[black,->,thick] (3,0)--(3,2) node[right] {$\underset{\sim}{v}(0)$};
\draw[black,->,thick] (3,0)--(1,0) node[midway,above] {$\underset{\sim}{a}(0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
A particle moves on the path shown, with position vector $\underset{\sim}{r}(t)=4\cos 3t\,\underset{\sim}{i}+4\sin 3t\,\underset{\sim}{j}$.
Show that its speed is constant, and state that speed.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=0.6cm, y=0.6cm]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-5.6,0) -- (5.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-5.6) -- (0,5.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0) circle (4);
\node[below left] at (-4,0) {$-4$};
\node[below right] at (4,0) {$4$};
\node[above left] at (0,4) {$4$};
\node[below left] at (0,-4) {$-4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
